  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nNear Term Forecast...\nThe current surface analysis places a cold front along the I-70\ncorridor, with expected progression southeastward and out of \nour CWA by mid-afternoon. RAP analysis continues to highlight an\nunstable airmass ahead of the boundary, characterized by MLCAPE\nvalues near 3000 J/kg and deep-layer shear profiles of 30-40 \nkt. Given this overlap, a brief window of opportunity for severe\nthunderstorm development exists through mid afternoon before \nthe front exits the region. Behind the boundary, a cooler and \ndrier air mass is filtering into the region on gusty northwest \nwinds. Dew points have already begun a downward trend, \neventually bottoming out in the 50s across the northern tier of \nthe forecast area tonight, with ambient temperatures settling \ninto the 60s under mostly clear skies. \n\nWednesday through Friday...\nThe middle of the week will feature a transition to a more \ntranquil regime as a robust Canadian high-pressure system \nbuilds southeast across the Midwest. This will facilitate a \ntrend toward below-normal temperatures, with afternoon highs \nconfined to the mid to upper 70s. Dry conditions are anticipated\nto prevail through Thursday night. By Friday, guidance suggests\nthe potential for warm air advection-driven convection \noriginating to our west. While confidence remains low, there is \na non-zero potential for these showers and thunderstorms to \nencroach upon portions of central Illinois; however, the lack of\nsignificant moisture return currently limits more robust PoP \ninclusion. \n\nThis Weekend into Next Week...\nAs the surface ridge translates toward the Atlantic Coast over \nthe weekend, the return of low-level southerly flow will \ninitiate a noticeable warming trend. Temperatures are progged to\nrebound significantly, with widespread 90s returning to the \nforecast by Sunday and Monday. The synoptic pattern remains \nanchored by northwest flow aloft, with periodic, lower-amplitude\nshortwaves expected to traverse the region. These disturbances \nmay serve as catalysts for convective initiation, though timing \nand placement remain difficult to resolve this far out. Have \ncapped precipitation chances at approximately 20% during this \ntimeframe until better agreement is reached among short-range \nguidance.",
